00:00:46.081 --> 00:00:47.500
This is a small audience,

00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:50.300
so feel free to interrupt
and ask questions since

00:00:50.301 --> 00:00:51.950
I'm treating this more as

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:54.765
a seminar thing even though
the talk that's recorded.

00:00:54.766 --> 00:00:57.080
So, today, I'm going
to tell you about

00:00:57.081 --> 00:00:59.840
non-convex matrix completion is

00:00:59.841 --> 00:01:03.310
a joint work with Rong
from Duke University.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.310
So in recent years,

00:01:06.311 --> 00:01:09.650
non-convex optimization has
become quite successful in

00:01:09.651 --> 00:01:13.760
practice like multi-layer
neural networks

00:01:13.761 --> 00:01:17.045
becomes the dominating
approach to

00:01:17.046 --> 00:01:19.220
many problems in computer vision

00:01:19.221 --> 00:01:22.395
and speech recognition
and many other things.

00:01:22.396 --> 00:01:24.215
However, the theoretical analysis

00:01:24.216 --> 00:01:27.745
of non-convex optimization
still lags behind.

00:01:27.746 --> 00:01:30.440
Recently, there has been a lot

00:01:30.441 --> 00:01:32.690
of attention to try to improve

00:01:32.691 --> 00:01:36.240
our understanding to
non-convex approaches

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:38.580
governed by this question.

00:01:38.581 --> 00:01:40.040
Basically, can we explain

00:01:40.041 --> 00:01:43.440
why they work very
well in practice?

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.675
Some of the question that
you can ask in this regime,

00:01:47.676 --> 00:01:49.910
for example you could ask are

00:01:49.911 --> 00:01:53.270
all the local optima
approximately global,

00:01:53.271 --> 00:01:56.870
optimal for some given
objective function?

00:01:56.871 --> 00:02:00.470
Can you understand
the structural geometry

00:02:00.471 --> 00:02:01.760
of the objective function?

00:02:01.761 --> 00:02:04.585
Can you use that to
design faster algorithms?

00:02:04.586 --> 00:02:10.830
So, half of this talk belongs
to non-convex optimization.

00:02:10.831 --> 00:02:12.335
The other half belongs to

00:02:12.336 --> 00:02:16.310
spectral graph theory for
lack of a better name.

00:02:16.311 --> 00:02:19.190
So, this is a field that bridge

00:02:19.191 --> 00:02:22.175
together discrete and
continuous optimizations,

00:02:22.176 --> 00:02:26.015
so if you look at
two separate fields,

00:02:26.016 --> 00:02:28.745
one is like graph theory
which studies

00:02:28.746 --> 00:02:33.125
discrete objects which has
combinatorial algorithms.

00:02:33.126 --> 00:02:36.275
Studies graph trees paths flows.

00:02:36.276 --> 00:02:40.160
The other, you have
numerical analysis towards where

00:02:40.161 --> 00:02:41.960
people do iterative
algorithms look

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:45.300
at matrices and Eigenvectors.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:48.350
Recently, there has been

00:02:48.351 --> 00:02:50.270
quite a lot of
success in combining

00:02:50.271 --> 00:02:51.860
these two fields to produce

00:02:51.861 --> 00:02:53.870
faster algorithms
for luxury close,

00:02:53.871 --> 00:02:57.920
say solving STD linear
systems max-flow,

00:02:57.921 --> 00:03:01.100
better solver for
linear programming

00:03:01.101 --> 00:03:02.595
and many, many other problems.

00:03:02.596 --> 00:03:04.040
So, the other half of

00:03:04.041 --> 00:03:06.680
this talk will belong to
spectral graph theory.

00:03:06.681 --> 00:03:11.330
So, this is roughly where
this work comes in,

00:03:11.331 --> 00:03:12.895
a high-level big picture.

00:03:12.896 --> 00:03:16.800
So, now I'm going to dive into

00:03:16.801 --> 00:03:19.775
the problem definition and

00:03:19.776 --> 00:03:22.950
what I actually want to do
about matrix completion.

00:03:22.951 --> 00:03:25.510
So, in the matrix
completion problem,

00:03:25.511 --> 00:03:28.680
you have unknown n
by n matrix of rank

00:03:28.681 --> 00:03:31.790
r. The input to

00:03:31.791 --> 00:03:34.870
the problem is a subset
of entry reveal to you,

00:03:34.871 --> 00:03:37.490
sometimes people assume as

00:03:37.491 --> 00:03:41.490
uniformly at random independently
with some probability.

00:03:41.491 --> 00:03:44.360
Your goal is look at
those entries and try

00:03:44.361 --> 00:03:47.900
to recover this low-rank matrix.

00:03:47.901 --> 00:03:53.120
Okay. This is a basic
machine learning problem

00:03:53.121 --> 00:03:55.310
that has applications
to recommendation

00:03:55.311 --> 00:03:57.470
systems and
collaborative filtering.

00:03:57.471 --> 00:03:59.645
For example, you could think of

00:03:59.646 --> 00:04:03.065
the rows of the matrix as
users and columns as movies.

00:04:03.066 --> 00:04:04.380
If you observe entry,

00:04:04.381 --> 00:04:08.340
it means in your system
a user rated movie,

00:04:08.341 --> 00:04:10.800
gave some score to that movie.

00:04:10.801 --> 00:04:14.330
The underlying low-rank
factorization just

00:04:14.331 --> 00:04:15.980
means every movie has

00:04:15.981 --> 00:04:19.640
some features and every user
has their preferences.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.030
So, throughout this talk,

00:04:23.031 --> 00:04:23.810
we're going to assume

00:04:23.811 --> 00:04:26.390
the underlying matrix
is incoherent.

00:04:26.391 --> 00:04:29.360
That is basically to avoid
this degenerate case

00:04:29.361 --> 00:04:33.590
where it's only a very sparse
matrix, somewhere is one.

00:04:33.591 --> 00:04:35.360
So, the assumption just says

00:04:35.361 --> 00:04:38.290
all the singular values
should have similar norms,

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:40.670
and we're going to assume

00:04:40.671 --> 00:04:43.175
all the observations
are noiseless.

00:04:43.176 --> 00:04:45.355
So, no noise added,

00:04:45.356 --> 00:04:47.385
just try to recover this matrix.

00:04:47.386 --> 00:04:51.980
Okay? So traditionally,
the most classic approach to

00:04:51.981 --> 00:04:54.440
this problem at least with

00:04:54.441 --> 00:04:57.350
theoretical understanding
is convex relaxation.

00:04:57.351 --> 00:05:01.040
So, you can formulate
this as minimizing

00:05:01.041 --> 00:05:03.110
the nuclear norm of
the matrix subject

00:05:03.111 --> 00:05:06.230
to that matrix agree with
all the observation you have.

00:05:06.231 --> 00:05:07.850
Okay? So, nuclear norm is

00:05:07.851 --> 00:05:11.210
just the sum of
the singular values of n. So,

00:05:11.211 --> 00:05:14.375
what he actually wanted to
do is to minimize the rank

00:05:14.376 --> 00:05:15.590
of the matrix subject

00:05:15.591 --> 00:05:18.040
to this constraint but
this is simply hard.

00:05:18.041 --> 00:05:20.475
So instead of minimizing

00:05:20.476 --> 00:05:23.120
the L0 norm of
your singular values,

00:05:23.121 --> 00:05:24.840
you just do L1 norm instead.

00:05:24.841 --> 00:05:28.520
Okay? You can show that
if all the entries are

00:05:28.521 --> 00:05:30.380
observed uniformly at random

00:05:30.381 --> 00:05:33.145
and with sufficient probability,

00:05:33.146 --> 00:05:36.515
then this convex program

00:05:36.516 --> 00:05:38.700
will give you the exact solution,

00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:41.115
will recover m. It can be solved,

00:05:41.116 --> 00:05:44.120
it can be basically written as

00:05:44.121 --> 00:05:47.960
SDP and solved in
time n to the four.

00:05:47.961 --> 00:05:51.495
Okay. Not fast
enough in practice,

00:05:51.496 --> 00:05:52.980
so we're not happy with that.

00:05:52.981 --> 00:05:55.450
That's why in practice,

00:05:55.451 --> 00:05:59.380
mostly people use a
non-convex heuristics.

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:03.860
The most intuitive non-convex
objective function

00:06:03.861 --> 00:06:07.250
is just to solve for
a variable X that has

00:06:07.251 --> 00:06:11.720
the same underlying dimension
than the low-rank factor

00:06:11.721 --> 00:06:13.550
and you just want to minimize

00:06:13.551 --> 00:06:17.750
the least squared error
where's the observations.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.800
So, there has been a lot of

00:06:21.801 --> 00:06:23.810
work on trying to understand why

00:06:23.811 --> 00:06:26.030
a non-convex approaches works

00:06:26.031 --> 00:06:28.345
very well for matrix
completion in practice.

00:06:28.346 --> 00:06:33.875
So, in the beginning that
the earlier works focus on

00:06:33.876 --> 00:06:36.380
what happens if I can

00:06:36.381 --> 00:06:40.160
have careful initialization
mostly from SVD.

00:06:40.161 --> 00:06:41.930
So basically, there
are a lot of work

00:06:41.931 --> 00:06:44.030
show that if you start

00:06:44.031 --> 00:06:47.930
at the right place then
run gradient descent,

00:06:47.931 --> 00:06:50.760
you're going to end up
at the ground-truth.

00:06:50.761 --> 00:06:55.310
The later, there are
more work giving a framework

00:06:55.311 --> 00:06:57.625
based-approach for this
problem that showed

00:06:57.626 --> 00:07:00.690
strong convexity near
the global optimum,

00:07:00.691 --> 00:07:04.075
so that a lot of the algorithm
will actually work.

00:07:04.076 --> 00:07:06.810
More recently, people
have been looking

00:07:06.811 --> 00:07:10.985
at doing just gradient descent

00:07:10.986 --> 00:07:13.550
without some special
initialization bases

00:07:13.551 --> 00:07:14.990
start anywhere and

00:07:14.991 --> 00:07:19.100
to show that this function
has no bad local optima.

00:07:19.101 --> 00:07:21.680
I think Tony Mao was
here presenting some of

00:07:21.681 --> 00:07:26.505
the work about two years ago,
a year half ago.

00:07:26.506 --> 00:07:28.970
Right. So basically,
these are some of

00:07:28.971 --> 00:07:30.875
the previous work on

00:07:30.876 --> 00:07:33.835
convex and non-convex
matrix completion.

00:07:33.836 --> 00:07:36.845
But the crucial thing
which motivated

00:07:36.846 --> 00:07:41.005
our work is that all of
these papers on this slide,

00:07:41.006 --> 00:07:46.445
these assume that all the entries

00:07:46.446 --> 00:07:50.860
are revealed to you with
the same probability. Okay?

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:53.700
In this talk, we just want to ask

00:07:53.701 --> 00:07:55.420
the following very
simple question.

00:07:55.421 --> 00:07:57.715
That is, what if

00:07:57.716 --> 00:08:01.930
they're not observed with
exactly the same probability.

00:08:01.931 --> 00:08:05.400
They're just observed was
at least probability P. This

00:08:05.401 --> 00:08:09.210
is more realistic
assumption in practice,

00:08:09.211 --> 00:08:12.980
because let's say
a certain group of your users

00:08:12.981 --> 00:08:14.180
might want to reveal

00:08:14.181 --> 00:08:15.490
a certain subset of items

00:08:15.491 --> 00:08:16.960
more often than the other people.

00:08:16.961 --> 00:08:21.080
For example, man likes to

00:08:21.081 --> 00:08:29.280
reveal- I don't know, something.

00:08:29.281 --> 00:08:31.660
And the woman likes to
review shoes or something.

00:08:31.661 --> 00:08:36.030
So, it's very surprising

00:08:36.031 --> 00:08:39.470
to me at least why people
haven't been asking,

00:08:39.471 --> 00:08:41.590
question this old assumption that

00:08:41.591 --> 00:08:44.440
the probability are
exactly the same.

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:47.370
On the other hand, this is

00:08:47.371 --> 00:08:50.485
actually very related
to another model,

00:08:50.486 --> 00:08:53.880
where semi random adversary

00:08:53.881 --> 00:08:56.860
can look at which samples
are revealed to you,

00:08:56.861 --> 00:08:58.865
and then reveal
additional entry to you,

00:08:58.866 --> 00:09:00.250
to try to introduce

00:09:00.251 --> 00:09:03.980
a bad local optima in
your objective function.

00:09:03.981 --> 00:09:07.545
Okay. So, the question we
want to answer here is,

00:09:07.546 --> 00:09:10.505
are the previous
non-convex approaches

00:09:10.506 --> 00:09:12.495
robust in their
semi-random model?

00:09:12.496 --> 00:09:16.125
And if not, is there a way
that you can fix them?

00:09:16.126 --> 00:09:19.660
But also still still

00:09:19.661 --> 00:09:22.960
make them faster than
they're convex approaches.

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:29.880
So, from now on this talk
will be these three parts.

00:09:29.881 --> 00:09:32.565
So, first, we're
going to show that

00:09:32.566 --> 00:09:35.330
two counter examples to

00:09:35.331 --> 00:09:38.950
the the previous
non-conscious approaches,

00:09:38.951 --> 00:09:41.340
basically tell you that it's

00:09:41.341 --> 00:09:44.105
possible that they can get
stuck in a bad local optima,

00:09:44.106 --> 00:09:45.760
and even if you want to do

00:09:45.761 --> 00:09:47.865
careful initialization with SVD,

00:09:47.866 --> 00:09:50.145
It might give you a
totally wrong subspace.

00:09:50.146 --> 00:09:52.920
Then, we're going to introduce
a pre-processing step,

00:09:52.921 --> 00:09:56.390
that tries to reweight

00:09:56.391 --> 00:09:59.940
the entries so that
the input becomes random.

00:09:59.941 --> 00:10:01.750
Basically tried to undo what

00:10:01.751 --> 00:10:03.705
the adversary did to the input.

00:10:03.706 --> 00:10:05.970
Then, eventually I'm
going to prove to you

00:10:05.971 --> 00:10:08.375
that after this
pre-processing step,

00:10:08.376 --> 00:10:12.370
that the non-convex
objective function

00:10:12.371 --> 00:10:14.299
has no bad local optima.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:19.090
Okay. Good. So, now let me
first do the counter examples.

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:26.380
So, this is the recall the
non-convex objective function.

00:10:26.381 --> 00:10:29.410
So, the counter example
just works like this.

00:10:29.411 --> 00:10:32.080
I'm writing them as
the two-by-two matrices,

00:10:32.081 --> 00:10:34.220
but they're actually
block matrices.

00:10:34.221 --> 00:10:36.910
So, each one should be multiplied

00:10:36.911 --> 00:10:40.695
by all ones matrix of size
of over two by over two.

00:10:40.696 --> 00:10:45.200
P is the probability that
you observe this entry.

00:10:45.201 --> 00:10:47.860
Okay. Basically, you're
much more likely to

00:10:47.861 --> 00:10:50.510
observe entries from
these diagonal blocks,

00:10:50.511 --> 00:10:52.970
than the off diagonal blocks.

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:59.445
X is the bad local optima that
I'm going to get stuck at,

00:10:59.446 --> 00:11:02.180
which is roughly
equal to this number.

00:11:02.181 --> 00:11:08.350
Okay. So, the way

00:11:08.351 --> 00:11:11.810
we can verify that there is
a bad local optima near X,

00:11:11.811 --> 00:11:12.960
is to basically say,

00:11:12.961 --> 00:11:15.750
what's the expectation of

00:11:15.751 --> 00:11:17.825
M* condition on
the revealed entries?

00:11:17.826 --> 00:11:20.160
Okay. Because these entries

00:11:20.161 --> 00:11:22.095
are revealed with
much higher probability,

00:11:22.096 --> 00:11:23.645
this is what you

00:11:23.646 --> 00:11:26.240
essentially get if you
take the expectation.

00:11:26.241 --> 00:11:29.525
When all the entries are
observed with same probability,

00:11:29.526 --> 00:11:32.595
this expectation is
exactly equal to M*.

00:11:32.596 --> 00:11:34.840
But now it's different.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:37.220
That's essentially the reason why

00:11:37.221 --> 00:11:38.730
you can take this matrix,

00:11:38.731 --> 00:11:41.535
and plug-in that expectation of

00:11:41.536 --> 00:11:45.125
first order derivative
of f(x) is zero,

00:11:45.126 --> 00:11:47.700
and the hash in the PSD.

00:11:47.701 --> 00:11:51.750
That means if P is high enough,

00:11:51.751 --> 00:11:56.065
then there will be
bad local optima near X.

00:11:56.066 --> 00:11:59.350
Roughly speaking, the reason
why this is happening is

00:11:59.351 --> 00:12:04.145
because the sampling is biased.

00:12:04.146 --> 00:12:07.575
So that the actual thing
that your observed,

00:12:07.576 --> 00:12:13.310
is biased because of P.
You becomes so local.

00:12:13.311 --> 00:12:14.960
Sorry, this should be X.

00:12:14.961 --> 00:12:18.650
So, X becomes a bad local optima

00:12:18.651 --> 00:12:21.915
because it's eigen
vector of this matrix.

00:12:21.916 --> 00:12:23.990
Is not rigorously true,

00:12:23.991 --> 00:12:28.510
but that's roughly what's
going on here. Yes.

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:33.465
&gt;&gt; Why do we have
expectation of M*?

00:12:33.466 --> 00:12:37.380
&gt;&gt; Because this is

00:12:37.381 --> 00:12:39.405
the objective function
that you are using-

00:12:39.406 --> 00:12:40.940
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:49.060
&gt;&gt; Correct. Basically, you
can take the expectation of

00:12:49.061 --> 00:12:52.680
f(x) and the probability

00:12:52.681 --> 00:12:55.780
of ij in that's observed is given

00:12:55.781 --> 00:12:59.885
by the big P matrix. That's why-

00:12:59.886 --> 00:13:03.340
&gt;&gt; I guess the thing I
don't understand is,

00:13:03.341 --> 00:13:06.910
the fact that this is
not bad local optima,

00:13:06.911 --> 00:13:09.690
is high probability statement?

00:13:09.691 --> 00:13:11.795
&gt;&gt; Yes. When P is high enough,

00:13:11.796 --> 00:13:13.840
then- first of all,

00:13:13.841 --> 00:13:16.275
if all the samples are
given uniformly at random,

00:13:16.276 --> 00:13:19.760
when P is high enough, you
don't have that local optima.

00:13:19.761 --> 00:13:23.665
&gt;&gt; I just don't get how
two by two example-

00:13:23.666 --> 00:13:28.120
&gt;&gt; It is not two by
two. They're 2n by 2n,

00:13:28.121 --> 00:13:29.995
and I'm just writing-

00:13:29.996 --> 00:13:32.035
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] understand.
Can you expalin again?

00:13:32.036 --> 00:13:34.670
&gt;&gt; Right. So, basically
all of these matrices

00:13:34.671 --> 00:13:37.050
are n by n. But I'm writing out,

