00:16:38.911 --> 00:16:41.850
Okay, good. So, now

00:16:41.851 --> 00:16:44.470
let me tell you about
our preprocessing step.

00:16:44.471 --> 00:16:49.890
Basically, we will look at
the first counter-example

00:16:49.891 --> 00:16:52.415
and ask ourselves,
what went wrong?

00:16:52.416 --> 00:16:54.290
So what happens is, if

00:16:54.291 --> 00:16:56.640
all the entries are revealed
with the same probability,

00:16:56.641 --> 00:16:59.550
this is roughly what you
expect to see from a matrix.

00:16:59.551 --> 00:17:01.395
But in that counter-example,

00:17:01.396 --> 00:17:04.460
the diagonal blocks are much
more likely to be observed

00:17:04.461 --> 00:17:05.700
than the off diagonal blocks,

00:17:05.701 --> 00:17:08.010
so you see something like this.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:12.910
Let's say if the adversary
tells you that he's

00:17:12.911 --> 00:17:15.770
changing the probability like
this, then what can you do?

00:17:15.771 --> 00:17:20.115
Can you do something to
recover mSTAR, exactly?

00:17:20.116 --> 00:17:22.190
One thing you can do is,

00:17:22.191 --> 00:17:26.750
you can pretty much just
resample this diagonal block.

00:17:26.751 --> 00:17:28.990
For example, you just
keep roughly 10 percent

00:17:28.991 --> 00:17:30.605
of the elements here.

00:17:30.606 --> 00:17:33.600
Then what you get
is essentially like

00:17:33.601 --> 00:17:36.435
a uniform sample,
or more generally,

00:17:36.436 --> 00:17:38.175
you can try to put
different weights

00:17:38.176 --> 00:17:39.890
on the samples that you observe,

00:17:39.891 --> 00:17:41.830
so that the weight that you get

00:17:41.831 --> 00:17:43.190
from the diagonal and off

00:17:43.191 --> 00:17:44.875
diagonal blocks are
roughly the same.

00:17:44.876 --> 00:17:48.380
If you do this, essentially,

00:17:48.381 --> 00:17:50.030
it's like your samples
are coming from

00:17:50.031 --> 00:17:52.550
a uniform random distribution,
and then you'll be fine.

00:17:52.551 --> 00:17:55.580
All right.

00:17:55.581 --> 00:17:57.110
So, the key idea of

00:17:57.111 --> 00:17:58.800
our preprocessing algorithm is

00:17:58.801 --> 00:18:00.070
basically to re-weight
the sample,

00:18:00.071 --> 00:18:02.190
so it looks like a random input,

00:18:02.191 --> 00:18:03.935
and what do I mean by that?

00:18:03.936 --> 00:18:04.990
First of all,

00:18:04.991 --> 00:18:09.195
this is a weighted, non-commerce,
objective function.

00:18:09.196 --> 00:18:13.700
So, if you take the graph
view of this matrix,

00:18:13.701 --> 00:18:17.030
and then if I put an edge
between node I and node J,

00:18:17.031 --> 00:18:18.465
if that entry is observed,

00:18:18.466 --> 00:18:21.680
this matrix basically
corresponds like this graph.

00:18:21.681 --> 00:18:25.320
So, it's an Erdos-Renyi graph
with probability p,

00:18:25.321 --> 00:18:27.970
but then the versary added

00:18:27.971 --> 00:18:29.650
some additional edge to

00:18:29.651 --> 00:18:33.440
form these two very
dense sub-graphs.

00:18:33.441 --> 00:18:36.430
Our algorithmic task here,

00:18:36.431 --> 00:18:42.575
it's just to recover
a Erdos-Renyi random graph.

00:18:42.576 --> 00:18:44.140
Basically, this is a graph

00:18:44.141 --> 00:18:45.930
that's like expander
plus two clicks,

00:18:45.931 --> 00:18:49.295
we just want to find
some expander from that graph.

00:18:49.296 --> 00:18:51.160
That's exactly the problem we

00:18:51.161 --> 00:18:54.980
solved in the preprocessing step.

00:18:55.410 --> 00:19:00.460
Questions? All right.

00:19:00.461 --> 00:19:02.530
So, the graph formulation
is following,

00:19:02.531 --> 00:19:05.335
that you're given a graph G,

00:19:05.336 --> 00:19:06.370
that you know contains

00:19:06.371 --> 00:19:08.815
a subgraph that's similar
to some other graph H,

00:19:08.816 --> 00:19:10.410
and you want to reweight G,

00:19:10.411 --> 00:19:13.030
so that the weighted version
is spectrally similar

00:19:13.031 --> 00:19:15.760
to H. For matrix completion,

00:19:15.761 --> 00:19:19.085
H is just a complete graph
or an expander.

00:19:19.086 --> 00:19:22.150
This problem, if you stare at it,

00:19:22.151 --> 00:19:23.455
is very similar to a

00:19:23.456 --> 00:19:26.010
well-established problem
in spectral graph theory,

00:19:26.011 --> 00:19:28.235
that is Graph Sparsification.

00:19:28.236 --> 00:19:29.840
So, Graph Sparsification is

00:19:29.841 --> 00:19:31.680
a problem that gave
you a dense graph,

00:19:31.681 --> 00:19:34.360
that you want to produce
some weighted sparse graph,

00:19:34.361 --> 00:19:39.185
that have the same property,

00:19:39.186 --> 00:19:41.140
say commute time,

00:19:41.141 --> 00:19:45.860
and a lot of other things,
with the original graph.

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:50.805
It's been first proposed
by Spielman and Teng,

00:19:50.806 --> 00:19:53.120
where they showed that if

00:19:53.121 --> 00:19:54.840
you want two graphs
to be Epsilon close,

00:19:54.841 --> 00:19:57.890
then you need to take an
over Epsilon squared edges,

00:19:57.891 --> 00:19:59.840
this still their highest
polylog factors,

00:19:59.841 --> 00:20:01.840
and M is number of edges,

00:20:01.841 --> 00:20:04.900
N is the number of nodes.

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:07.640
Then, a beautiful work of Batson,

00:20:07.641 --> 00:20:08.670
Spielman, and Srivastava,

00:20:08.671 --> 00:20:10.690
they showed that you
can actually get away

00:20:10.691 --> 00:20:14.890
with an over Epsilon
square edges this tight,

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:17.920
and the running time has then

00:20:17.921 --> 00:20:20.240
been improved to
nearly linear time.

00:20:20.241 --> 00:20:21.570
So, the algorithm we're using in

00:20:21.571 --> 00:20:23.150
this paper for the preprocessing

00:20:23.151 --> 00:20:27.930
is directly inspired by

00:20:27.931 --> 00:20:30.690
the recent advancement
on this problem.

00:20:30.691 --> 00:20:32.580
Now, I'm going to show you

00:20:32.581 --> 00:20:36.475
the formal formulation
for these two problems.

00:20:36.476 --> 00:20:41.855
Hopefully, you should be
convinced at their core,

00:20:41.856 --> 00:20:43.905
they are essentially the same.

00:20:43.906 --> 00:20:47.905
So, this is the spectral
sparsification problem.

00:20:47.906 --> 00:20:50.330
I'm basically taking the vectors,

00:20:50.331 --> 00:20:53.695
and then multiplying each
of them by L inverse half,

00:20:53.696 --> 00:20:58.030
that's inverse half power
of the Laplacian,

00:20:58.031 --> 00:21:00.030
so that the sum becomes identity.

00:21:00.031 --> 00:21:02.860
So the spectral
sparsification problem is,

00:21:02.861 --> 00:21:04.470
I give you a bunch of

00:21:04.471 --> 00:21:07.515
rank one matrices,
whose sum is identity,

00:21:07.516 --> 00:21:12.440
and I want you to find a set
of sparse coefficients,

00:21:12.441 --> 00:21:15.620
so that their sum is
approximately identity.

00:21:15.621 --> 00:21:17.880
The problem is
solved. The problem

00:21:17.881 --> 00:21:19.595
we want to solve in this picture,

00:21:19.596 --> 00:21:21.160
is to say that,

00:21:21.161 --> 00:21:23.740
I give you a set of vector,

00:21:23.741 --> 00:21:26.820
and I promise you, there
exists a subset of vectors.

00:21:26.821 --> 00:21:29.790
That they sum up to
approximately 2i,

00:21:29.791 --> 00:21:32.525
and now I asked you to
find a set of weights,

00:21:32.526 --> 00:21:36.565
so that I don't have
the sparsity constraint,

00:21:36.566 --> 00:21:39.810
but I don't know
this hidden subsets as well.

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:42.530
You can see that there are

00:21:42.531 --> 00:21:45.300
some differences between
these two problems.

00:21:45.301 --> 00:21:47.570
But, I'm going to explain to you

00:21:47.571 --> 00:21:50.820
why some of the solution

00:21:50.821 --> 00:21:52.340
for the first problem
can be applied

00:21:52.341 --> 00:21:54.525
to our problem, and some cannot.

00:21:54.526 --> 00:21:59.170
Hopefully, that will be
useful for you guys.

