to handle our problem,

00:27:32.646 --> 00:27:34.980
because the argument that

00:27:34.981 --> 00:27:37.540
good edge exists hold
it for us as well.

00:27:37.541 --> 00:27:41.420
In each situation, we know
a good edge exists as well.

00:27:41.421 --> 00:27:44.235
Because if you look at
a subset of the edges,

00:27:44.236 --> 00:27:46.330
the average on that
subset is good.

00:27:46.331 --> 00:27:48.270
Even though we don't
know that's subset,

00:27:48.271 --> 00:27:50.265
but we know a good edges
still exists.

00:27:50.266 --> 00:27:52.080
So, you can basically just apply

00:27:52.081 --> 00:27:56.825
BSS directly on our problem
and get away with it.

00:27:56.826 --> 00:28:00.075
If I promise you the initial,

00:28:00.076 --> 00:28:02.710
if I change this
condition to identity,

00:28:02.711 --> 00:28:04.540
not approximately identity, then

00:28:04.541 --> 00:28:06.250
you can just apply
BSS to our problem.

00:28:06.251 --> 00:28:09.025
Then, you would solve this.

00:28:09.026 --> 00:28:13.430
Good. So, the
conceptual contribution

00:28:13.431 --> 00:28:17.090
of the preprocessing part
of our paper is basically,

00:28:17.091 --> 00:28:20.920
BSS is actually more
powerful than just

00:28:20.921 --> 00:28:22.840
applying it to graph
sparcification

00:28:22.841 --> 00:28:25.445
is actually can be applied.

00:28:25.446 --> 00:28:27.360
We believe it can be applied very

00:28:27.361 --> 00:28:30.100
broadly to a lot of
semi-random settings.

00:28:30.101 --> 00:28:33.605
The technical contribution
is basically two things.

00:28:33.606 --> 00:28:35.880
One is that, like I said,

00:28:35.881 --> 00:28:40.345
the total sum of your vector
is no longer identity.

00:28:40.346 --> 00:28:42.485
So, for example, that
prevents you from

00:28:42.486 --> 00:28:44.750
using some of the
sparcification techniques.

00:28:44.751 --> 00:28:49.305
The other is, we need
to go in and basically

00:28:49.306 --> 00:28:51.570
adapt the analysis so that it

00:28:51.571 --> 00:28:55.105
