00:38:10.530 --> 00:38:14.710
Okay. Now, I'm just
going to conclude.

00:38:14.711 --> 00:38:20.390
So, basically our main results
are are threefold.

00:38:20.391 --> 00:38:23.630
First is we pose and study

00:38:23.631 --> 00:38:26.910
non-convex matrix completion
and basic question

00:38:26.911 --> 00:38:30.180
this commonly used assumption

00:38:30.181 --> 00:38:31.530
that Pij equal to p and we'll

00:38:31.531 --> 00:38:33.505
say is at least p. What happens?

00:38:33.506 --> 00:38:35.450
We shall counter examples that

00:38:35.451 --> 00:38:38.365
the existing non-convex
approach would not work.

00:38:38.366 --> 00:38:41.830
Then in light of
this negative results,

00:38:41.831 --> 00:38:45.065
we propose a
preprocessing problem

00:38:45.066 --> 00:38:46.290
and we showed that you can solve

00:38:46.291 --> 00:38:47.885
this in nearly linear time

00:38:47.886 --> 00:38:51.615
and get some good
spectral property

00:38:51.616 --> 00:38:54.955
from your weight matrix.

00:38:54.956 --> 00:38:56.410
Then using that property,

00:38:56.411 --> 00:38:58.730
you can show that
the non-convex objective

00:38:58.731 --> 00:39:00.525
has no bad local optima.

00:39:00.526 --> 00:39:03.210
So, you can just run
gradient descent or

00:39:03.211 --> 00:39:06.430
alternative minimization on
it and you will be fine.

00:39:06.431 --> 00:39:12.820
Good. So, the other main
conceptual contribution

00:39:12.821 --> 00:39:15.020
of this work is basically
we're trying to bridge

00:39:15.021 --> 00:39:18.070
together to relatively
young field.

00:39:18.071 --> 00:39:21.810
One is Spectral graph
theory that develops

00:39:21.811 --> 00:39:23.840
fast algorithms based
on the connection

00:39:23.841 --> 00:39:26.895
between discrete and
continuous optimization,

00:39:26.896 --> 00:39:30.330
and the other is
a theoretical analysis

00:39:30.331 --> 00:39:32.220
of non-convex algorithms.

00:39:32.221 --> 00:39:34.510
Basically, in this work
we showed that you

00:39:34.511 --> 00:39:37.075
can get efficient algorithm

00:39:37.076 --> 00:39:39.520
by mostly using non-commercial
approach but run

00:39:39.521 --> 00:39:43.055
a very lightweight convex
optimization in the beginning.

00:39:43.056 --> 00:39:46.770
You sort of, like get
better outcomes both.

00:39:46.771 --> 00:39:49.850
Good. Okay. Open problems,

00:39:49.851 --> 00:39:51.290
so are two of them.

00:39:51.291 --> 00:39:54.085
One is that actually,

00:39:54.086 --> 00:39:56.640
this should be exact recovery.

00:39:56.641 --> 00:40:00.590
There's something that I'm
sweeping under the rug here.

00:40:00.591 --> 00:40:02.520
Previous results were able to

00:40:02.521 --> 00:40:04.365
show that if P is large enough,

00:40:04.366 --> 00:40:06.710
you can actually
exactly recover M star.

00:40:06.711 --> 00:40:09.900
But in our people who
actually we can show that,

00:40:09.901 --> 00:40:11.765
and I have a counter example

00:40:11.766 --> 00:40:14.375
of that actually being false.

00:40:14.376 --> 00:40:16.920
So, you cannot
recover it exactly,

00:40:16.921 --> 00:40:18.340
you can only recover it up to

00:40:18.341 --> 00:40:20.565
some epsilon Frobenius Norm.

00:40:20.566 --> 00:40:23.840
Behind that is really
this question is,

00:40:23.841 --> 00:40:26.510
how much samples do the adversary

00:40:26.511 --> 00:40:29.420
needs to reveal to
you so that you can

00:40:29.421 --> 00:40:31.880
actually get stuck in
local minima that's

00:40:31.881 --> 00:40:34.990
close to the global optimum?

00:40:34.991 --> 00:40:36.805
We don't know the answer
to this question.

